# What must never reach the public site.
#
# THIS FILE LIVES AT THE ASSET ROOT, AND THAT IS NOT COSMETIC.
#
# Until 2026-08-16 `wrangler.toml` said `[assets] directory = "."`, so the asset root WAS
# the repository root and this file sat beside it. That PR changed the directory to
# `public/`, and the question "where does wrangler then look for the denylist?" was not
# established anywhere — it was measured, because guessing it wrong publishes source.
#
# MEASURED, wrangler 4.123.0, `WRANGLER_LOG=debug wrangler deploy --dry-run`, on a probe
# tree carrying a denylist in BOTH places and one victim file named by each:
#
#   ✨ Read 6 files from the assets directory <root>/public
#   /.assetsignore
#   /denied-by-assetroot.txt
#   /denied-by-reporoot.txt
#   /index.html
#   /sub
#   /sub/keep.txt
#   Ignoring asset: .assetsignore
#   Ignoring asset: denied-by-assetroot.txt
#
# The file named by `public/.assetsignore` was ignored. The file named by the REPOSITORY-ROOT
# `.assetsignore` was NOT — it published. wrangler reads the denylist at
# `<assets.directory>/.assetsignore` and nowhere else, and its patterns anchor to the asset
# root, so `/denied-by-assetroot.txt` is the address a pattern must match — not
# `public/denied-by-assetroot.txt`.
#
# So a denylist left at the repository root after that change is not a weakened control. It
# is an INERT one: read by nothing, while still reading like protection to anyone who opens
# it. That is worse than no file at all, and it is why this file moved with the boundary.
#
# Patterns are gitignore-style, anchored at THIS directory. .assetsignore itself is never
# uploaded (wrangler applies that rule implicitly — see the probe output above).
#
# NOTE for whoever edits this next: _redirects and _headers must NOT be listed here.
# Wrangler consumes them from the uploaded asset set, so ignoring them silently
# disables every redirect rule they define.
#
# ── THE 2026-08-19 RULING: ten rules removed, and the test that decided each one ───────
#
# The 2026-08-16 boundary move left this file full of rules naming paths that had just moved
# OUT of the asset root. That PR kept them deliberately and said so, on the reasoning that a
# rule matching nothing costs nothing and a consolidation might re-arm it. mp#86 re-raised
# the whole list as an open question; JOB Z is the ruling, and it goes the other way. Ghost's
# merge of the JOB Z card IS the ruling — one decision, the whole list, one commit.
#
# WHY a rule matching nothing is not free: a denied file never enters the publish set, so
# door 1 (`.github/ci/publish-set.mjs`) never sees it and never calls it UNEXPECTED. A
# denylist entry is therefore a BLINDFOLD on door 1 as well as a shield. For anything that
# could only arrive here by a deliberate commit, the shield is redundant — door 1 already
# turns red on any committed path that is not in the expected set — and the blindfold is the
# only part left. Removing those entries makes the gate catch MORE, never less.
#
# THE TEST, applied to every one of the seventeen rules that were here, against the real
# `public/` tree (all seventeen matched nothing in it — that measurement alone decides
# nothing, which is why the test is about HOW a match could ever arrive):
#
#   REMOVED — the rule names a committed repository artifact that lives outside `public/`.
#   A copy inside the asset root could only get here by someone committing it, and door 1
#   fails that commit by name. Ten rules: .github · .gitignore · README.md · LICENSE ·
#   package.json · wrangler.toml · retired-values.json (it lives in .github/ci/ since
#   mp#86) · workers · design-system/ci · design-system/og-template.html.
#
#   KEPT — the rule names an artifact NOTHING commits: a tool, an OS, or a local run puts
#   it on disk. `wrangler deploy` uploads what is on DISK, and a developer deploying from
#   their own machine never passes through CI, so door 1 cannot see these and this file is
#   the only control there is. Seven rules, below.
#
# Both halves are verified, not asserted: door 1 reports the identical publish set before
# and after this edit — 79 paths, 79 expected, 0 unexpected, 0 STALE — because none of the
# ten removed rules was denying anything. What changes is the future, and only in the
# direction of visibility.

# ── Kept: artifacts nothing commits ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
.git
node_modules
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# Wrangler's local state directory. It is gitignored, so it never exists on a CI checkout —
# but `wrangler deploy` uploads what is on DISK, and a developer who has run `wrangler dev`
# in this tree has one. If it is ever created inside the asset root it would publish local
# cache and miniflare SQLite files; this denies it in both spellings.
.wrangler
**/.wrangler

# npm writes this next to wherever it is run, without anyone committing it. The repository's
# own manifest lives at the repository root and is gated by door 1 if it ever appears here.
package-lock.json
